        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Ancient Greek religion</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1400 BCE – c. 500 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> world: gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> are named on Mycenaean tablets of the 14th and 13th centuries BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">None; its myths took their classic form in the poems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> around 700 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">No sacred book: the epics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a>, the Homeric Hymns and the sacred laws and calendars of each city</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">None as an ancient tradition: the Christian emperors banned its sacrifices in the 390s; a modern revival was recognised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 2017</span></div>
