    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> worshipped many gods, led by the twelve Olympians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, together with heroes, nymphs and local powers, through sacrifice, prayer, festivals and oracles, with no sacred book and no single priesthood to define belief.</li><li>Religion was woven into the life of every city: its temples, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>, its festivals, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panathenaea">Panathenaia</a> and the Olympic Games, and its drama, born in the festivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>.</li><li>Banned by the Christian emperors in the 390s, it left myths, told by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> and the tragedians, that became one of the foundations of Western art and literature.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The ancient Olympic Games were a religious festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>: athletes swore an oath before a statue of the god, and a hundred oxen were sacrificed at his great altar in the middle of the festival.</p></aside>
    </section>

