    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hesiod"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hesiod.jpg" alt="Hesiod" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Hesiod_(%3F)-Ancient_Roman_busts_in_the_Neues_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yair Haklai, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Greek religion grew from many roots. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, in the 2nd millennium BCE, worshipped goddesses in palaces, caves and on mountain peaks, with symbols such as the double axe and the bull. On the mainland the Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> recorded offerings to their gods on clay tablets in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> script, deciphered in 1952: among the names are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, and a “Mistress of Athana” who may be an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>.</p><p>Some of these gods were inherited from the Indo-European ancestors of the Greeks: Zeus, the sky father, shares his name and nature with the Roman Jupiter and the Vedic Dyaus Pita. Others came from or were shaped by the older peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and the civilisations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>’s myth of the succession of the gods resembles Hurrian and Hittite myths of the kingship in heaven, and Aphrodite has much in common with the Near Eastern goddess Ishtar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>.</p><p>After the collapse of the Mycenaean palaces around 1200 BCE, writing disappeared from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> for some four centuries, but the worship of the gods continued. With the rise of the city-states in the 8th century BCE, sanctuaries grew: the first temples were built to house images of the gods, and great sanctuaries shared by all Greeks developed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>, Delphi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodona">Dodona</a>. By tradition the first Olympic Games, in honour of Zeus, were held in 776 BCE.</p><p>The Greeks themselves believed that their gods had been given their familiar forms by poets. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> wrote, in the 5th century BCE, that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and Hesiod, some four hundred years before his own time, had taught the Greeks the genealogy of the gods, given them their titles and described their appearance. The epics of Homer and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a> of Hesiod, composed around 700 BCE, became the shared foundation of Greek religion and myth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zeus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zeus.jpg" alt="Zeus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zeus_Otricoli_Pio-Clementino_Inv257.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> worshipped many gods, who were immortal, powerful and human in form and passions. The twelve Olympians, who lived on Mount Olympus, were led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, god of the sky and father of gods and men, with his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>; his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, god of the sea and earthquakes; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a>, goddess of grain; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, goddess of wisdom, war and crafts; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, god of prophecy, music and healing; his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>, the huntress; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>, god of war; Aphrodite, goddess of love; Hephaestus, the smith; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a>, the messenger; and Hestia, goddess of the hearth, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, god of wine and ecstasy. Hades ruled the underworld with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a>.</p><p>Beyond the Olympians, the Greek world was full of divine powers: nymphs of springs and trees, river gods, the Fates, the Furies, and heroes, mortals of the distant past such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, who were worshipped at their tombs and could help or harm the living. Every city had its own protecting gods and local cults, and the same god could have very different characters in different places.</p><p>The gods were not creators of the world, which in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>’s account had arisen from Chaos, nor necessarily models of morality: the myths tell of their quarrels, love affairs and jealousies. They were, however, guardians of oaths, hospitality and justice, and they punished hubris, the arrogance of mortals who forgot their limits.</p><p>Religion was above all a matter of practice rather than belief: of honouring the gods correctly. Its central act was animal sacrifice, performed at an altar in front of the temple: the thigh bones wrapped in fat were burned for the gods, and the meat was shared and eaten by the worshippers. The Greeks also offered libations of wine, first fruits, cakes and votive gifts, and they prayed with arms raised, reminding the god of past offerings. Killing, birth and death brought pollution, miasma, which had to be cleansed by purification.</p><p>The Greeks sought the will of the gods through oracles, above all that of Apollo at Delphi, whose priestess, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythia">Pythia</a>, answered the questions of cities and individuals, and through seers who read the entrails of sacrificed animals and the flight of birds. Most believed that the dead lived on as shades in the gloomy realm of Hades, but the mystery cults, above all the Mysteries of Demeter and Persephone at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a>, offered their initiates the hope of a happier lot after death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theogony"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theogony.jpg" alt="Theogony" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hesiod,_Theogony,_Venice,_Gr._464.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hesiod, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Greek religion had no sacred book, no creed and no authoritative interpreter. Its stories of the gods were told by poets, whose works were known by every educated Greek, and its rules of worship were set by the custom and laws of each city.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, composed around the 8th or 7th century BCE, show the gods taking part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> and the wanderings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, intervening in human affairs, favouring their heroes and quarrelling on Olympus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a> tells the origin of the world and the gods, from Chaos and Earth to the overthrow of the Titans by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, who establishes his just rule; his Works and Days teaches the justice of Zeus and tells the story of Prometheus, who stole fire for humanity, and of Pandora.</p><p>The Homeric Hymns, composed between the 7th and 5th centuries BCE, celebrate individual gods; the Hymn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> tells the story of the abduction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a> that lay behind the Mysteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a>. The odes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> honoured victors at the sacred games, and the tragedies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a>, performed at the festivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, explored the relations between gods and mortals; Euripides’ Bacchae dramatises the arrival of Dionysus and the punishment of those who refuse him.</p><p>Inscriptions preserve the practical side of religion: sacred laws, festival calendars, lists of sacrifices, records of temple treasures and the inscribed cures of the healing god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepius">Asclepius</a> at Epidaurus. Gold tablets placed in graves from around 400 BCE give instructions to the souls of initiates on their journey through the underworld, and the Derveni <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a>, a commentary on an Orphic poem charred on a funeral pyre in the 4th century BCE, is the oldest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The travel writer Pausanias, in the 2nd century CE, described the sanctuaries and cults of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in detail.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/paestum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paestum.jpg" alt="Paestum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/paestum">Paestum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veduta_di_Paestum_2010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oliver-Bonjoch, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Greek religion travelled with Greek settlers, who from the 8th century BCE founded cities around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, from Marseille to Cyrene and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. The temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paestum">Paestum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrigento">Agrigento</a> and Selinunte in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> are among the best preserved of the Greek world.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> readily adopted foreign gods and identified them with their own: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> equated Egyptian gods with Greek ones, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> welcomed the Thracian goddess Bendis and, in 420 BCE, the healing god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepius">Asclepius</a> from Epidaurus. Philosophers questioned the traditional gods: in the 6th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophanes">Xenophanes</a> mocked the idea of gods in human form, and in 399 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> was condemned to death in Athens for impiety. Others, such as the Stoics, read the myths as allegories of nature and moral truth.</p><p>The conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> carried Greek gods across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> appears in Buddhist art as a protector of the Buddha. Hellenistic kings were worshipped as gods, and new cults spread, such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapis">Serapis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> and Fortune.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> identified their gods with the Greek ones, Jupiter with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, and adopted Greek myths, art and temple forms, while the great sanctuaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> flourished under Roman rule; the emperor Hadrian was initiated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a>.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread, the old religion declined. The emperor Julian tried to revive it between 361 and 363, but in 391 and 392 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> banned pagan sacrifice and worship, and, by tradition, the last ancient Olympic Games were held in 393. Temples were closed, destroyed or turned into churches, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>. In 529 Justinian forbade pagans to teach, and the Platonic school of Athens closed. Worship of the old gods lingered in remote places, such as the Mani peninsula of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, until the 9th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/parthenon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parthenon.jpg" alt="Parthenon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Parthenon_in_Athens.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Steve Swayne, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Greek religion had no church and no professional clergy with a doctrine to teach. Priests and priestesses were usually citizens who served a particular god or sanctuary, chosen by election, by lot or from the families that held the priesthood by inheritance; they managed the sanctuary and performed its rituals, but magistrates carried out the sacrifices of the state, and the head of each household honoured the gods of the home, above all Hestia of the hearth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> the protector of property.</p><p>Religion gave women an important public role. The priestess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena-polias">Athena Polias</a> was one of the most prominent figures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythia">Pythia</a> at Delphi spoke for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, and festivals such as the Thesmophoria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> were reserved for women.</p><p>The city’s year was marked by festivals. In Athens the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panathenaea">Panathenaia</a> honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> with a procession, shown on the frieze of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>, which brought a new robe for her ancient statue, with athletic and musical contests; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysia">City Dionysia</a> brought new tragedies and comedies before the citizens. The great Panhellenic games, the Olympic, Pythian, Isthmian and Nemean, were festivals of Zeus, Apollo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, protected by a sacred truce.</p><p>A sanctuary consisted of a sacred precinct, an altar for sacrifice and often a temple, which housed the image of the god and the offerings given to it; worshippers gathered outside, at the altar, not in the temple. Cities and individuals filled sanctuaries with dedications, from small figurines to statues and treasuries.</p><p>Religious life also included healing at the sanctuaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepius">Asclepius</a>, where the sick slept in the hope of a dream cure, mystery initiations, private vows, curse tablets and magic. Impiety was a crime in Athens, and prosecutions for it, such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, show how closely religion was bound to the community.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ovid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ovid.jpg" alt="Ovid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ovid_by_an_anonymous_sculptor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucasaw, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Greek religion left one of the richest mythologies in the world. Through Roman poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>, its gods and heroes passed into medieval and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> literature and art, from Botticelli’s Birth of Venus to the operas, poems and novels of modern times, and its stories remain among the most widely known in the world.</p><p>Its sanctuaries created some of the masterpieces of architecture and sculpture: the temples of the Acropolis, Delphi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a> established the classical orders that shaped Roman architecture and, through the Renaissance and neoclassicism, the public buildings of the modern world.</p><p>Drama was born in the festivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, and the Olympic Games, revived in 1896, recall the athletic festivals held in honour of the gods. Words such as hero, mystery, theology, panic and music, from Pan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>, come from Greek religion, and the names of the planets, through their Roman equivalents, honour its gods.</p><p>Greek reflection on the gods also gave birth to philosophy and theology, as thinkers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophanes">Xenophanes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> sought a purer idea of the divine; their concepts shaped Jewish, Christian and Islamic thought. In modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> a small movement seeks to revive the worship of the ancient gods, legally recognised as a religion in 2017.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ancient Greek religion, from the Mycenaean age to the end of antiquity, worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, the Olympian gods, heroes and local powers through sacrifice, festivals, games, oracles and mysteries, without a sacred book or a priestly caste. Woven into the life of every city, it inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>, drama and the Olympic Games, and its myths remain one of the foundations of Western culture.</em></p>

